  "query_preview": "..\n\nA background of rising geopotential heights dominates the forecast \nover the next 24 hours as a longwave ridge builds in downstream of \nan amplified Pacific wave tracking over the Rockies. A weak \ndisturbance embedded within the ridge is noted in WV imagery across \nthe western Great Lakes this afternoon. Apart from a few light \nshowers that will be possible near the Ohio border, this wave will \npass overhead with little fanfare tonight given continued dry \nadvection within light low-level southerly flow. Mid-level cloud \ncover will remain prevalent, keeping temps mild in the mid 40s and \nlower 50s for an overnight low.\n\nLow pressure takes shape over the central/northern Plains on Monday \nwhile SE MI remains under the stable influence of deep layer \nridging. There is a brief window for mostly sunny skies in the \nmorning, then a mid-level shortwave emanating from the low helps \nusher in a ridge of sfc-700mb theta-e with increasing low cloud \ncover in the afternoon. Ascent within the saturated layer is weak \nbut enough confidence exists to warrant a 15 to 30% chance of light \nshowers or drizzle Monday evening and overnight.\n\nAnother period of height rises ensues daytime Tuesday as the mid-\nlevel wave closes off over the upper Midwest. This favors dry, \nbreezy, and very warm conditions on Tuesday as the upstream system \nushers in 850mb temps of 12 to 13C, which model soundings indicate \nmay be briefly mixed to. This sends highs into the mid 70s, some 15 \ndegrees above normal and near daily max temp records. Latest NBM \nadvertises a 40% chance to exceed 75 degrees for much of SE MI.\n\nThe mature system's warm conveyor belt inches in from the west early \nWednesday with models in agreement on light rainfall arriving by \nlate morning or early afternoon. 70 to 90% PoP looks good with \nguidance in line with QPF on the order of 0.25\". A narrow corridor \nof weak instability will exist just ahead of the cold front \nWednesday evening, supportive of an isolated rumble of thunder. With \nthe later frontal passage, high temps Wednesday will again be very \nmild in the lower 70s. By Thursday and Friday, progressively cooler \nconditions settle in amidst scattered light showers as the occluded \nlow meanders over the region.",
